# 13. PERFORMANCE AND RELIABILITY VALIDATION

Use existing benchmarks and safe evidence first.

Evaluate, where applicable:

- Cold model initialization.
- Warm inference latency.
- End-to-end processing latency.
- First-result latency, if streaming exists.
- Real-Time Factor.
- Throughput.
- Concurrent job capacity.
- Peak RAM.
- Memory growth.
- Recovery time.

Use:

RTF = Processing Wall-Clock Time / Input Audio Duration.

Separate inference time from initialization, preprocessing, queueing, and postprocessing.

For every measurement, record hardware, runtime, execution provider, model version, audio characteristics, repetition count, and measurement method.

Report individual observations. Use median or percentile statistics only when sample size supports them.

Compare before-and-after measurements under equivalent conditions.

Never extrapolate from a single run or invent performance targets.

Evaluate existing evidence for:

- Short and long audio.
- Silence, noise, low-volume, corrupted, and truncated inputs.
- Different sample rates and channels.
- Repeated processing.
- Multiple jobs within a project.
- Concurrent jobs across separate projects.
- Cancellation.
- Retry and interrupted-job recovery.
- Original-audio preservation.
- Temporary-file lifecycle.

This is a coverage requirement, not permission to execute every test.

Run only tests whose side effects and resource requirements have been assessed and controlled.

Do not perform unbounded stress tests, exhaust resources, or test production systems.

